# Git worktrees created by Claude Code live under .claude/worktrees/ and are
# full copies of the repo. nx must NOT scan them — otherwise every project is
# defined in two locations and the project graph fails with
# ProjectsWithConflictingNamesError. (.gitignore already lists .claude/, but
# .nxignore makes the intent explicit and independent of git-ignore semantics.)
.claude
# Same class: the repo convention (CLAUDE.md §Bash) puts agent worktrees at
# <project>/.worktrees/. Without this line, EVERY worktree breaks EVERY commit
# via the pre-commit `nx affected` hook (ProjectsWithConflictingNamesError) —
# proven live 2026-07-16 by .worktrees/agent-mcp-usage-accounting.
.worktrees
# apigen-cli's own test suite ships fake npm-registry fixtures with real
# package.json files (registry/pkg-a, pkg-b) to test its registry-reading
# code against realistic data. Nx's inferred-project detection treats ANY
# directory with a package.json as a project with no project.json required,
# so these get registered as real projects (`@test/pkg-a`, `@test/pkg-b`) —
# confirmed via `nx show projects`. That's harmless for build/test/lint (they
# have no targets), but `nx release version` walks every release-eligible
# project and interactively prompts for a version-bump type per project with
# no non-interactive fallback — with stdin unavailable (e.g. backgrounded),
# it hangs indefinitely with near-zero CPU, silently blocking any release run
# across the whole workspace. Discovered 2026-07-19 verifying the apigen
# release. Scoped to the whole fixtures dir so future pkg-c/pkg-d fixtures
# don't reintroduce this without an .nxignore update.
entrypoint/apigen-cli/src/test/fixtures
